# Keep sensitive security data out of GitHub

The user requires this policy for all TMApps projects and future updates.

- Never commit, push or upload client secrets, API secrets, passwords, access or
  refresh tokens, session cookies, authorization codes, private keys, signing
  certificates with private material, recovery codes, database credentials,
  service-role keys, credential stores or real user security data to GitHub.
  This applies to public and private repositories, branches and Git history.
- Apply the same restriction to release packages, installers, archives, documents,
  screenshots, videos, logs, CI artifacts, issue reports, PR text and comments.
  Do not print secret values in commands, tool output or diagnostics.
- Keep runtime credentials in the appropriate OS credential store or secret
  manager. Keep server credentials on the server. Use protected GitHub Actions
  secrets for authorized CI tasks; never bake them into client applications,
  public configuration, generated files or distributable packages.
- Keep local secret configuration and credential files untracked and ignored.
  An ignore rule does not remove an already tracked file or erase Git history.
  Commit templates with clearly fake placeholders instead of real credentials.
- Public OAuth client IDs and explicitly publishable keys are distinct from
  secrets. Include them only when their provider documents them as suitable for
  public clients; never treat a client secret, service-role key or privileged
  server key as publishable. Public values do not replace authorization checks.
- Before each commit, push or release, review the actual staged changes and any
  generated/uploaded artifacts for sensitive data. Use an available secret
  scanner with redacted output alongside manual review, and investigate findings
  before publishing. Never paste a suspected secret into GitHub to discuss it.
- If exposure is suspected, stop the affected publication and tell the user using
  redacted identifiers or file locations. Treat exposed credentials as compromised
  and arrange revocation/rotation. Removing a value from the latest file is not
  sufficient. Do not rewrite shared history or force-push without explicit user
  authorization; coordinate any history and release-asset cleanup safely.
- Use synthetic accounts and fake security data for tests and screenshots. Review
  crash reports and support exports before submission, including embedded files.
- Carry this rule forward when creating a new TMApps app, branch or project.
